<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Sahara</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1975 CE · capital El Aaiún</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's desert colony on the Atlantic coast of north-west Africa, whose hasty abandonment in 1975 left the unresolved conflict over Western Sahara.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the coast of Río de Oro, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakhla">Villa Cisneros</a>, now Dakhla. Agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between 1900 and 1912 fixed the borders, adding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguia-el-hamra">Saguia el-Hamra</a> to the north, but Spanish control remained confined to a few coastal posts for decades.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> occupied the interior only in 1934, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">El Aaiún</a> later became the capital. The Sahrawi population of nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic">Hassaniya</a>-speaking tribes lived largely by herding. In 1958 the territory became a Spanish province. The rich phosphate deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bou-craa">Bou Craa</a>, discovered in 1947, began to be exploited in the early 1970s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> launched an independence struggle in 1973, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> claimed the territory. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a> in November 1975, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> Accords handed administration to them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> withdrew in early 1976. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara's</a> status remains disputed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-sahara.jpg" alt="Spanish Sahara" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: VoodooIsland at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_Sahara.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>El Aaiún</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Western Sahara</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rio de Oro, Saguia el-Hamra and Rio de Oro, Spanish West Africa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=spanish-sahara" aria-label="Spanish Sahara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4335 1258 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Sahara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4655 1514L4909 989M4655 1514L4909 989M4655 1514L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4655 1514h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4655 1514h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=spanish-sahara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

